    "answer": {
      "short": "$P(x)=\\dfrac12(x-2)^2(x+1)$.",
      "worked": "The zeroes and their multiplicities give $P(x)=k(x-2)^2(x+1)$. Using the $y$-intercept,\n$$\\begin{align*}P(0)&=k(0-2)^2(0+1)\\\\2&=4k\\\\k&=\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $P(x)=\\dfrac12(x-2)^2(x+1)$. Its leading coefficient is positive, so $P(x)\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$, as required.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
